    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0447a62e-7975-505d-bb28-e7ff21da7ad9",
      ":question/problem": "Find the determinant of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nUse a Laplace expansion along row 4, then evaluate the triangular minor.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Write $D=\\det(A)$.\n\nExpand $D$ along row 4; only 1 nonzero entry contributes.\n\n$$\nD=(3)(-1)^{4+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWrite $D_{1}$ for the minor obtained by deleting row 4 and column 2.\n\nThe matrix in $D_{1}$ is triangular, so multiply its diagonal entries.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D_{1} &=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=(-2)\\cdot(3)\\cdot(1) \\\\ &=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these minor values,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D &=(3)(-1)^{4+2}(-6) \\\\ &=-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\det(A)=-18$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
